import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const keelingCfg = {
  points: [{
    points: [[1958, 315.2], [1970, 325.7], [1980, 338.8], [1990, 354.4], [2000, 369.6], [2010, 389.9], [2020, 414.2], [2024, 424.6]],
    label: 'CO₂ at Mauna Loa (annual mean)', mode: 'both', color: '#4d7c0f',
  }],
  x: { min: 1955, max: 2026, label: 'year' },
  y: { min: 300, max: 440, label: 'CO₂ (ppm)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};

const fluxCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'e', label: 'annual emissions', min: 2, max: 15, step: 0.5, value: 10, unit: 'GtC/yr' },
    { name: 'f', label: 'airborne fraction', min: 0.2, max: 0.6, step: 0.05, value: 0.45 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'carbon staying airborne', expr: 'e * f', unit: 'GtC/yr' },
    { label: 'yearly CO₂ rise', expr: 'e * f / 2.13', unit: 'ppm' },
    { label: 'years to add 100 ppm', expr: '100 / (e * f / 2.13)', unit: 'yr' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/foundational/carbon-cycle-climate">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Carbon is the skeleton of life and the currency of climate. Every living thing is built on
    carbon chains, and the planet trades carbon between four great reservoirs like a bank
    moving money between accounts: the <strong>atmosphere</strong> (~880 billion tonnes of
    carbon, GtC), the <strong>oceans</strong> (~38,000 GtC — the giant account), <strong>soils</strong>
    (~1,600 GtC) and <strong>living vegetation</strong> (~550 GtC). The flows between them —
    <em>fluxes</em> — are huge and balanced: photosynthesis pulls roughly 120 GtC out of the air
    every year, and respiration, decay and fire push almost exactly the same amount back. For
    ten thousand years the books balanced at about 280 ppm of atmospheric CO₂.
  </p>
  <p>
    Since about 1850, humans have been adding a bonus line to the ledger: burning fossil carbon
    — coal, oil, gas — that plants buried over hundreds of millions of years, at a rate of
    roughly <strong>10 GtC per year</strong> (about 37 billion tonnes of CO₂). Geology suddenly
    became an annual line item.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Keeling curve — direct CO₂ measurement at Mauna Loa since 1958; the sharpest turn in the record" widget="plotter" config={keelingCfg} />

  <p>
    Charles David Keeling started measuring CO₂ on Mauna Loa in 1958 and got 315 parts per
    million (ppm). The line has risen every year since and now passes 420 ppm — the highest in
    at least 800,000 years of ice-core record. The arithmetic of the curve is worth learning,
    because it recurs everywhere in climate science: each ppm is 2.13 billion tonnes of carbon,
    and only about <em>half</em> of what we emit stays airborne — oceans and land absorb the
    rest. The panel below lets you run the ledger yourself: set emissions and the airborne
    fraction, and read the yearly rise and how fast that adds up.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the emission ledger — emissions × airborne fraction, in the units that matter" widget="sliderLab" config={fluxCfg} />

  <h2>The four ledgers of the carbon cycle</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>vegetation ledger</strong> is the fast one: forests and plankton draw down
    carbon each growing season and exhale it each decay season — a heartbeat of months. The
    <strong>soil ledger</strong> is the slow one and easy to underestimate: dead roots and leaf
    litter hold about three times the carbon of all living plants, and draining peatlands or
    ploughing prairie releases it over decades. The <strong>ocean ledger</strong> is the
    colossus — CO₂ dissolves directly at the surface (cold water dissolves more, which is why
    warming <em>weakens</em> this sink) and is exported to depth by sinking organic matter and
    cold, dense polar water. It has quietly absorbed roughly a quarter of all human emissions,
    at a price: dissolved CO₂ forms carbonic acid, and surface-ocean pH has slipped from about
    8.2 to 8.1 — a 30% jump in acidity that stresses shell-building life.
  </p>
  <p>
    And beneath the fast cycle runs the <strong>geological cycle</strong>: volcanism exhales
    carbon from the deep Earth at only about 0.1–0.2 GtC per year, while rock weathering and
    the burial of marine shells and plankton pull carbon down just as slowly. That trickle
    matters because it is the original climate thermostat — over millions of years it
    compensated for a Sun 4% fainter in Earth's youth and set the ancient CO₂ levels — but it
    is a thermostat with geological hands: it cannot respond to a century-scale jolt, which is
    precisely what fossil burning delivered.
  </p>

  <h2>The greenhouse effect: the mechanism</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the air itself: 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, 0.9% argon — and only about 0.042%
    CO₂. How can a trace gas matter at all? Because of what it <em>does</em> with light. The
    Sun's energy arrives mostly as visible light, which N₂, O₂ and CO₂ all ignore; it warms
    the surface, and the surface re-radiates that energy as <strong>infrared</strong>. Here
    nitrogen and oxygen stay passive, but CO₂, water vapour and methane absorb IR strongly —
    their molecules flex and vibrate at exactly those wavelengths — and re-emit the energy in
    all directions, including back down. More of these gases means a thicker optical blanket.
    The effect is not hypothetical: without any greenhouse effect, Earth's average surface
    temperature would be about −18 °C instead of +15 °C; the natural greenhouse effect is
    worth 33 degrees, and the argument is strictly about the <em>extra</em> thickness we are
    adding.
  </p>
  <p>
    The physics was measured before the industrial revolution finished: Eunice Foote (1856)
    and John Tyndall (1859) demonstrated CO₂'s heat-trapping in the laboratory; Svante Arrhenius
    calculated in 1896 that doubling CO₂ would warm the planet by several degrees. The modern
    numbers: the doubling of CO₂ since pre-industrial times (280 → 420+ ppm, not yet doubled)
    has already pushed global average surface temperature roughly 1.2–1.5 °C above the
    late-1800s baseline, with 2024 the hottest calendar year on record. The 2015 Paris
    Agreement set holding warming "well below 2 °C", aiming for 1.5 °C — the Keeling curve
    above is what those numbers fight against.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why are scientists confident the added CO₂ is <em>ours</em>? Three fingerprints. Fossil
    carbon is ancient, so it is depleted of carbon-13 and contains no carbon-14 — and
    atmospheric ratios of both isotopes are shifting exactly as burning old carbon predicts
    (the "Suess effect"). Atmospheric oxygen is declining in lock-step with the CO₂ rise,
    as combustion consumes it. And ice cores show 800,000 years in which CO₂ never left the
    180–300 ppm band — until the last two centuries.
  </p>
  <p>
    The system also answers back. Warmer air holds more water vapour — itself a greenhouse
    gas — which amplifies warming; melting ice replaces white, reflective surface with dark,
    absorptive ocean (the <em>ice–albedo feedback</em>); thawing permafrost releases methane;
    warmer oceans absorb <em>less</em> CO₂. Feedbacks, not linearity, are why the problem
    deserves the whole field of environmental science — and why the response of clouds, the
    hardest feedback to model, remains the largest single uncertainty.
  </p>

  <h2>Acid rain and ozone: the other two atmospheric wounds</h2>
  <p>
    Climate change is not the only way combustion rewrote the sky. <strong>Acid rain</strong>
    (4.3) comes from sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides released by coal burning and vehicle
    engines; in the atmosphere they oxidise into sulphuric and nitric acid and fall with rain.
    Natural rain is already mildly acidic (pH ≈ 5.6, from dissolved CO₂), but polluted
    precipitation reached pH 4 and below — ten to a hundred times more acidic — acidifying
    lakes (killing fish eggs), damaging forests, and dissolving limestone buildings and
    statues at visible rates. The fixes were engineering: flue-gas scrubbers on power plants,
    catalytic converters on cars, and the 1990 US Clean Air Act Amendments, which capped SO₂
    emissions with a tradable-permit market. Acid rain is now the environmental movement's
    clearest success story: emissions fell over 80%, lake chemistry is recovering, and the
    episode proved that global-scale air problems are solvable when the mechanism is clear
    and the measurement honest.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Ozone depletion</strong> (4.4) is a different mechanism with a happier ending — so
    far. Stratospheric ozone (O₃) is not pollution but the planet's sunscreen, absorbing the
    UV-B that causes skin cancer and cataracts. In the 1970s chemists (Molina and Rowland,
    later Nobel laureates) predicted that chlorofluorocarbons — miraculous, inert refrigerants —
    would survive to the stratosphere where UV would crack them open and release chlorine;
    a single chlorine atom then catalytically destroys up to ~100,000 ozone molecules before
    retiring. Ground-based measurements found the proof in 1985: springtime ozone over
    Antarctica down by a third, the "hole" driven by polar-stratospheric clouds that turbocharge
    chlorine chemistry. The 1987 Montreal Protocol phased CFCs out worldwide — the most
    successful environmental treaty ever signed — and the hole is slowly healing, with recovery
    expected around mid-century. One caution for exam accuracy: "good up high, bad nearby" —
    stratospheric ozone protects, while ground-level ozone (a component of smog) damages lungs
    and crops. Same molecule, opposite verdicts, different layers.
  </p>

  <h2>The atmosphere in layers: where each story happens</h2>
  <p>
    The atmosphere is not a uniform blanket but a stack of layers with different jobs, and
    each problem in this chapter lives on its own floor. The <strong>troposphere</strong>
    (surface to ~12 km, thinner at the poles, deeper at the equator) holds 75–80% of the
    air's mass and essentially all its water: weather happens here, and so does the
    greenhouse effect's blanket — the CO₂, water vapour and methane are concentrated low,
    and temperature falls with altitude at about 6.5 °C per km, which is why mountaintops
    are cold. Above it the <strong>stratosphere</strong> runs warmer with height because its
    ozone absorbs UV directly — this is the ozone layer's home (peak concentration around
    20–25 km), and the reason ozone depletion and acid rain and climate change are three
    different stories with three different addresses: acid rain is a <em>tropospheric</em>
    washout problem, ozone a <em>stratospheric</em> photochemistry problem, climate change a
    whole-column radiation problem. Above that, the mesosphere burns up meteors, and the
    thermosphere hosts the aurora and the ISS; by 100 km (the Kármán line) the air is too
    thin for wings. One number to keep: 90% of the atmosphere lies below about 16 km — a
    layer, on the football-field scale of the geography lesson, thinner than the grass.
  </p>

  <h2>The ledger at personal and planetary scale</h2>
  <p>
    The emission arithmetic scales down to you, and the numbers are worth having. A typical
    person in a wealthy economy emits on the order of 10 tonnes of CO₂-equivalent a year
    (including imports and shared infrastructure); the global average is around 6; much of
    sub-Saharan Africa sits under 1. The largest personal lines are usually heating, driving
    and flying — one round-trip transatlantic economy flight is roughly a tonne of CO₂ per
    passenger, about a tenth of an average year in a single afternoon — followed by food
    (meat-heavy diets roughly double the food line) and everything wearing the embedded
    emissions of imports. Zoom back out and the distribution is the ethical headline: the
    wealthiest tenth of humanity is responsible for close to half of all emissions, while
    the poorest half contribute barely a tenth — one population, one atmosphere, and the
    ledger's biggest balances and smallest ones held by different people. Every climate
    policy is, underneath, an argument about which of these lines to move first and who
    pays for moving it.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The core quantitative question in climate science is <strong>sensitivity</strong>: how much
    does Earth warm per doubling of CO₂ once all feedbacks play out? Charney's 1979 estimate —
    "near 3 °C" — has survived four decades of scrutiny; the IPCC AR6 range is 2.5–4 °C, best
    estimate 3 °C. That number converts straight into the <em>carbon budget</em>: humanity can
    emit only a finite tonnage for a given temperature goal, and at 10+ GtC per year the
    remaining budget for 1.5 °C is spent within decades. This is why the ledger widget above
    is more than arithmetic — every international negotiation is, underneath the diplomacy,
    two sides arguing about that panel's inputs.
  </p>
  <p>
    The deeper questions are where research lives. <strong>Tipping elements</strong> — the
    Atlantic circulation (AMOC), the West Antarctic ice sheet, permafrost carbon — may have
    thresholds past which change becomes self-sustaining and effectively irreversible on human
    timescales; their locations are unknown. <strong>Negative emissions</strong> — burning
    biomass with carbon capture, or direct-air capture machines — now appear in most 1.5 °C
    pathways at gigatonne scale, and do not yet exist at that scale; how much of the plan rests
    on unproven technology is a live ethical question. And solar geoengineering (scattering
    sunlight with stratospheric aerosols) would cut temperatures within months at the cost of
    masking rather than curing, with governance that does not exist. Carbon moved civilisation
    from firewood to the moon; the last chapter of the carbon cycle — whether the ledger is
    rebalanced in time — is being written now, in units of parts per million.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NOAA Global Monitoring Laboratory (Mauna Loa data); Global Carbon Budget; IPCC Sixth Assessment Report (2021); Molina & Rowland, Nature (1974); Farman et al., Nature (1985); US EPA Acid Rain Program data.</p>
</TopicPage>
